<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Slow-start vehicle-type override for startup-lost-time experiments.
  Loaded via additional-files when slow_start is true.

  Design choice: tau=1.8s + actionStepLength=1.5s + sigma=0.5
  ============================================================
  Startup lost time in real traffic comes from two mechanisms:
    1. Reaction delay: time to perceive green + decide to accelerate.
    2. Headway gap: minimum following distance at queue discharge.

  SUMO's Krauss model has three relevant parameters:

  tau (minimum time headway):
    Default 1.0s -> saturation flow ~2930 veh/h (unrealistically high).
    tau=1.8     -> saturation flow ~1776 veh/h (HCM range: 1700-2200).
    HCM default saturation flow: 1900 veh/h/ln (headway ~1.89s).

  actionStepLength (reaction delay between decisions):
    Default 1.0s (= step length) means vehicles react instantly each step.
    actionStepLength=1.5 means vehicles only re-evaluate speed/acceleration
    every 1.5s, directly modeling the perception-reaction delay that causes
    startup lost time at green lights. This is the key parameter for the
    "first vehicle takes longer to go" effect.

  sigma (driver imperfection):
    Default 0.5 (Krauss) randomly varies acceleration to model driver
    inconsistency. Setting sigma=0.5 explicitly ensures stochastic variation
    in reaction times, matching field observations (ElDessouki & AlManeea 2023).

  Combined effect:
    - First vehicle at green waits ~1.5s to react (actionStepLength) instead
      of reacting instantly.
    - Each subsequent vehicle maintains ~1.8s headway (tau) instead of ~1.0s.
    - Over a 4-vehicle queue: ~2s extra startup lost time per phase (HCM: 2.0s).
    - Saturation flow drops from ~2930 to ~1776 veh/h (HCM: 1900).

  Justification:
    - HCM startup lost time default: 2.0s per phase.
    - HCM saturation flow default: 1900 veh/h/ln.
    - Field calibration (Chowdhury & Chakraborty 2024): tau=1.4 normal, higher
      under distraction.
    - ElDessouki & AlManeea (2023): smartphone usage increases startup lost
      time beyond HCM 2.0s default.
    - Auerbach et al. (arXiv:2307.11357): tau identified as key sim-to-real
      transfer gap for TSC policies.
    - UNECE AEBS: driver reaction 0.86-1.39s normal, higher under surprise;
      1.5s actionStepLength models the perception-decision-motor chain.
    - SUMO docs: "actionStepLength works similar to a reaction time" and
      "a value larger than the reaction time may induce collisions" (hence
      tau=1.8 > actionStepLength=1.5 keeps driving safe).

  Topology agnostic: overrides the "pkw" vType used by all three networks
  (grid4x4, manhattan_28x7, cologne1) without changing route files.
  All other vType params stay identical to baseline.
  Agents stay unchanged: they see lane counts/pressure, never tau.
-->
<additional xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
            xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="http://sumo.dlr.de/xsd/additional_file.xsd">

    <vType id="pkw" length="5.0" width="2.0" minGap="2.5"
           maxSpeed="11.111" accel="2.0" decel="4.5"
           tau="1.8" sigma="0.5" actionStepLength="1.5"
           color="1.0,0.5,0.0"/>
</additional>
